Exactly. And the connection is behavior doesn’t merely respond to selection pressures. Behavior can change the selection pressures themselves.

That’s an established evolutionary concept called niche construction. Organisms modify their environments, which then changes what behaviors and traits are advantageous. PubMed Central (PMC) +1

Now introduce algorithms, AI agents, and wireless communication, and you get several interacting feedback loops.

There’s an important distinction with Wi-Fi signaling:

* Transmission: Wi-Fi can carry information that connected devices turn into messages, imagery, sounds, and other experiences.
* Sensing: Radio signals can reveal aspects of physical behavior. Research underlying IEEE 802.11bf demonstrates motion, presence, and environmental sensing through changes in wireless signals. NIST +1
* Behavioral adaptation: Algorithms can use collected signals to modify the content someone encounters, potentially influencing subsequent behavior.
* Selection: Repeated behavior can alter social and cultural selection pressures; over generations, environmental changes can also influence biological evolution.

But Wi-Fi sensing motion is fundamentally different from reading someone’s private thoughts or directly inserting thoughts into their brain. Ordinary Wi-Fi does not establish those capabilities.

What interests me about your connection is the possibility of recursive niche construction across biological and digital systems:

Organisms and agents

Generate behavior

Sensors and algorithms

Measure, filter, classify

Modified environments

Information, opportunities, incentives

New selection pressures

Favor different behaviors and adaptations

Feedback into the next cycle

The fascinating part is that the selection mechanism itself can evolve.

An algorithm selects content. People adapt their behavior to the content. Their behavior trains or informs subsequent algorithms. Those algorithms reshape the environment again.

Over time, you’re not merely selecting within an environment. You’re modifying the environment that performs the selection.

And the distinction between cultural evolution, machine learning, and biological evolution becomes especially important because they operate on very different timescales and inheritance mechanisms.

The environment becomes part of the evolutionary machinery.

One thing I want to distinguish in your Wi-Fi point, though: are you thinking primarily about wireless sensing of behavior, or about projecting information and stimuli into an environment to influence behavior? They’re two different mechanisms, but they can form parts of the same feedback system.